{"run_id": "gpt_A01_api_r1", "input_file": "inputs/learning/A_joelfixes/A01_before.txt", "input_words": 45, "input_sha256": "fcd55d537346353d18f9495e9186eaeda6773210978cc8b3309e02cbc05ed4ef", "allocation": "learning", "started_utc": "2026-09-29T02:20:05.415902+00:00", "status": "completed", "balance_before": 57837, "provider_id": "26a7fe31-d49f-45df-98ff-a94e748ea42c", "words_charged": 45, "style": {"transport": "API", "requested": "Auto", "resolved_style": "not exposed by API"}, "output_file": "runs/emulate/gpt_A01_api_r1.txt", "output_words": 95, "output_sha256": "355b1d353d869e4cad3663cceef9a9265982c49e5e1a7a08e2f25b18da286a0a", "response": {"id": "26a7fe31-d49f-45df-98ff-a94e748ea42c", "tool": "humanize", "text": "This song by Celine is named “On ne change pas”. Which means “we don’t change” And I believe it’s true. When we grow up, we do not stop being the child we were at the beginning. So many things that happen to us when we’re grown-up touch us as we were still children, and it makes us feel as vulnerable, as unimportant, as lonely, as helpless, as impotent, as impulsive, as scared, as incapable, as in despair, as in the need to find someone to pick us above all the others and to love us…", "reads_human": null, "words": {"charged": 45}, "elapsed": 5}, "balance_after": 57792, "notes": "Authorized lossless API fallback; exact provider text, no editing."}
{"run_id": "gpt_A02_api_r1", "input_file": "inputs/learning/A_joelfixes/A02_before.txt", "input_words": 114, "input_sha256": "395833196e47c58f84cde8dd6b92a9c2932c93c3d2428294424c71c4c8d30aa1", "allocation": "learning", "started_utc": "2026-09-29T02:22:40.250817+00:00", "status": "completed", "balance_before": 57792, "provider_id": "50433e88-2655-4469-aa2f-f532d77c7404", "words_charged": 114, "style": {"transport": "API", "requested": "Auto", "resolved_style": "not exposed by API"}, "output_file": "runs/emulate/gpt_A02_api_r1.txt", "output_words": 112, "output_sha256": "ef23320fa94f4a38c2fc767fa3dc34cc41b93c909474e5bcf1dd8851fe47b2bf", "response": {"id": "50433e88-2655-4469-aa2f-f532d77c7404", "tool": "humanize", "text": "Most of these instructions are assuming that you really do just want to take care of the imps child. But the truth is some people like the power of frightening something and a few would like the job to work better after therapy. So it is possible that you ignored some of the above and are thinking “I can just have the child out for a second”. And then you get your kick out of the child being scared and in fear. But all you really did was feed some part of yourself that wanted it to be scared. So if this is you then please leave the child alone for now.", "reads_human": null, "words": {"charged": 114}, "elapsed": 5}, "balance_after": 57678, "notes": "Authorized lossless API fallback; exact provider text, no editing."}
{"run_id": "gpt_A03_api_r1", "input_file": "inputs/learning/A_joelfixes/A03_before.txt", "input_words": 69, "input_sha256": "4eea025dc05054027b8ec26c7573b36df30450e1b77554b421dd7b130eb10368", "allocation": "learning", "started_utc": "2026-09-29T02:22:52.538660+00:00", "status": "completed", "balance_before": 57678, "provider_id": "1d0b0dad-9c84-43b0-b1ef-8f867f386f00", "words_charged": 69, "style": {"transport": "API", "requested": "Auto", "resolved_style": "not exposed by API"}, "output_file": "runs/emulate/gpt_A03_api_r1.txt", "output_words": 66, "output_sha256": "59a008d2aac5362e3d8ff46045d57a2e730a4d0f59ee29a8733cca166a6d924d", "response": {"id": "1d0b0dad-9c84-43b0-b1ef-8f867f386f00", "tool": "humanize", "text": "It’s important to point out that in this post I am assuming that you want to be good to your inner child. It is possible that you might enjoy scaring your inner child, or belittling it, or want to “fix” it so it becomes a better worker bee. If that’s the case, you need to deconstruct your adult self first, before embarking on inner child therapy.", "reads_human": null, "words": {"charged": 69}, "elapsed": 3}, "balance_after": 57609, "notes": "Authorized lossless API fallback; exact provider text, no editing."}
{"run_id": "gpt_A04_api_r1", "input_file": "inputs/learning/A_joelfixes/A04_before.txt", "input_words": 57, "input_sha256": "d382882f14100a8f1c0908c5eb9a0b2b9fc372da88432ba624b83388cc395d44", "allocation": "learning", "started_utc": "2026-09-29T02:23:02.369818+00:00", "status": "completed", "balance_before": 57609, "provider_id": "508c25aa-14c1-4a99-9de5-034f17435c43", "words_charged": 57, "style": {"transport": "API", "requested": "Auto", "resolved_style": "not exposed by API"}, "output_file": "runs/emulate/gpt_A04_api_r1.txt", "output_words": 65, "output_sha256": "27d04b957d16772b18ea97214863c93e28cdeaa6f639a940b2049e6b29814890", "response": {"id": "508c25aa-14c1-4a99-9de5-034f17435c43", "tool": "humanize", "text": "What you can do if they still don't love you Yet, you can at least not make it worse by: Continuing to insult them Telling them what to do Attempting to hurry them up Expecting them to be grateful for having paid to sit through your session It won't make them trust you, but at least it'll show them you can be an adult sometimes.", "reads_human": null, "words": {"charged": 57}, "elapsed": 3}, "balance_after": 57552, "notes": "Authorized lossless API fallback; exact provider text, no editing."}
{"run_id": "gpt_A05_api_r1", "input_file": "inputs/learning/A_joelfixes/A05_before.txt", "input_words": 82, "input_sha256": "1cdb5871a347f5bba1b38a34b7e9fd23cbdbb5d67af8c2f91eb262ad3ae7a16e", "allocation": "learning", "started_utc": "2026-09-29T02:23:11.180981+00:00", "status": "completed", "balance_before": 57552, "provider_id": "d608c2b2-8282-4f4d-bd32-2f92aada191f", "words_charged": 82, "style": {"transport": "API", "requested": "Auto", "resolved_style": "not exposed by API"}, "output_file": "runs/emulate/gpt_A05_api_r1.txt", "output_words": 93, "output_sha256": "4d97077e62a078aa79df1c86f7c5e529f91f1ef0a60110d39790d47a220b99cb", "response": {"id": "d608c2b2-8282-4f4d-bd32-2f92aada191f", "tool": "humanize", "text": "What would a Minimally Competent Adult do to take care of that? It’s usually something really simple like eat or go to bed. Sometimes it’s something like, “Don’t send that text.” If the place you’re in is not safe, sometimes you need to leave the place you’re in, or lock the door and get help. Even if you feel like a child as you are doing this, that’s ok. You can explain to the little one afterwords that you had to do this for them, and that this is what the Protector does.", "reads_human": null, "words": {"charged": 82}, "elapsed": 5}, "balance_after": 57470, "notes": "Authorized lossless API fallback; exact provider text, no editing."}
